  - isSingular(W) -> line 4 of `/workspace/dataset/test15_executable "W"`
  - demodulize("Module::" + W) -> line 5 of `/workspace/dataset/test15_executable "W"`

Run MANY probes (hundreds). Use bash loops. Quote arguments properly. Capture stderr too (2>&1 separately from stdout when it matters) and capture exit codes.
Be systematic: form a hypothesis, then design inputs that DISCRIMINATE between competing hypotheses, then confirm.
Report the exact rule as precise pseudocode operating on BYTES (the C++ code uses std::string = bytes; the "C" locale means std::toupper/tolower only affect ASCII a-z/A-Z). Explicitly test non-ASCII UTF-8 input to confirm byte-level behavior.


=== ADVERSARIAL CROSS-CHECK: underscore ===

Another engineer probed the `underscore` behavior of /workspace/dataset/test15_executable and produced this spec. Your job is to FALSIFY it. Assume it is subtly wrong. Design and run inputs specifically engineered to break it, then report every discrepancy between the spec's prediction and the binary's actual output.

SPEC PSEUDOCODE:
// inflection::Inflector::underscore(const std::string& in) -> std::string
// Operates on raw BYTES. No allocation limit, no truncation, no state.
// Pure: identical input always yields identical output; independent of
// initialize(), of argv position, and of any pluralization/irregular-word rules.

string underscore(string in):
    string out = ""                       // starts empty
    for i = 0 .. in.size()-1:             // i is the BYTE INDEX into the INPUT
        byte c = in[i]
        if (c >= 0x41 && c <= 0x5A):      // 'A'..'Z' ONLY (ASCII uppercase, "C" locale)
            if (i > 0):                   // <-- condition is on the INPUT INDEX, not on out.empty()
                out += '_'                // 0x5F
            out += (byte)(c + 0x20)       // tolower: 'A'->'a' ... 'Z'->'z'
        else:
            out += c                      // every other byte copied verbatim, unchanged
    return out

// Equivalent one-liner: for each byte, an ASCII A-Z becomes "_" + lowercase,
// except at byte index 0 where the leading "_" is omitted. Nothing else changes.
//
// NOT done by this function (all explicitly disproven):
//   - no collapsing / de-duplication of adjacent or pre-existing '_' bytes
//   - no acronym grouping (HTMLParser -> h_t_m_l_parser, one '_' per capital)
//   - no "::" -> "/" translation (Rails-style) ; "::" is passed through as-is
//   - no '-' -> '_' translation ; no whitespace -> '_' translation
//   - no trimming, no leading/trailing '_' cleanup
//   - no digit/word-boundary insertion ("Test99" -> "test99", "99Test" -> "99_test"
//     only because 'T' is uppercase)
//   - no UTF-8 awareness; multi-byte sequences are copied byte-for-byte and each
//     of their bytes still consumes an index (so a capital after them gets a '_')


CLAIMED FINDINGS:
1. KEY DISCRIMINATOR RESOLVED: the guard is on the input BYTE INDEX (i > 0), not on whether the output buffer is empty and not on whether the previous char was a separator. Proof: "_A" -> "__a" (an output-empty rule would give "_a"; a previous-char-is-separator rule would also give "_a"). Corroborated by "-A" -> "-_a", " A" -> " _a", "__A" -> "___a", "_AB" -> "__a_b", "_A_" -> "__a_".
2. No collapsing/dedup of underscores whatsoever: "A_B" -> "a__b", "a_B" -> "a__b", "A__B" -> "a___b", "Already_Underscored" -> "already__underscored", "Ab_Cd_Ef" -> "ab__cd__ef". Already-lowercase snake_case is a fixed point: "already_underscored" -> "already_underscored".
3. Exhaustive single-byte sweep: probed "x<b>x" for every byte value b in 1..255 and dumped the output with od -tx1. EXACTLY bytes 0x41-0x5A ('A'-'Z') produced "x_<lower>x"; all 229 other byte values (0x01-0x40, 0x5B-0x7F, and every high byte 0x80-0xFF) were copied through unchanged. Byte 0x00 is unreachable via argv. This proves the classifier is plain ASCII isupper with the C locale and that high bytes are never treated as uppercase (no sign-extension surprise).
4. Non-ASCII UTF-8 passes through byte-for-byte (verified with od -tx1c): "Café" -> c3 a9 preserved -> "café"; "ÉCOLE" -> c3 89 5f 63 5f 6f 5f 6c 5f 65 (the É is untouched, and the following ASCII 'C' at byte index 2 DOES get a leading '_'); "日本語A" -> e6 97 a5 e6 9c ac e8 aa 9e 5f 61; "ÄÖÜ" -> unchanged c3 84 c3 96 c3 9c; "straße" -> unchanged. Invalid UTF-8 also passes through: "\xffZ" -> ff 5f 7a, "\xc3A" (lone lead byte) -> c3 5f 61.
5. Digits are ordinary pass-through bytes; underscores near digits come only from an adjacent capital: "A1B"->"a1_b", "a1B"->"a1_b", "1A"->"1_a", "99Test"->"99_test", "Test99"->"test99", "camelCase99Test"->"camel_case99_test".
6. Punctuation/whitespace are pure pass-through: "A-B"->"a-_b", "A.B"->"a._b", "A/B"->"a/_b", "A::B"->"a::_b", "A B"->"a _b", "A+B"->"a+_b", "A~B"->"a~_b", "A\tB"->"a\t_b", "A\nB"->"a\n_b" (verified via od, the embedded newline survives), "Hello World"->"hello _world", "hello-world"->"hello-world" (unchanged), "MyModule::MyClass"->"my_module::_my_class", "ActiveRecord::Errors"->"active_record::_errors". Notably it does NOT convert '::' to '/'.
7. Acronyms get one underscore per capital, no grouping: "ABC"->"a_b_c", "ABCD"->"a_b_c_d", "ABCDE"->"a_b_c_d_e", "HTMLParser"->"h_t_m_l_parser", "XMLHttpRequest"->"x_m_l_http_request", "getHTTPResponse"->"get_h_t_t_p_response", "SSLError"->"s_s_l_error", "ABCd"->"a_b_cd", "AbC"->"ab_c", "iOS"->"i_o_s", "IOs"->"i_os".
8. Empty string -> empty string (exit 0, verified with od: output line is just \n). Single chars: "A"->"a", "a"->"a", "Z"->"z", "z"->"z", "_"->"_", "__"->"__".
9. Long input has no truncation or limit. "AbC" x2000 (6000 bytes) -> 9999 bytes, exactly matching 4 + 1999*5. "A" x5000 -> 9999 bytes = 1 + 4999*2. Prefix/suffix spot-checks match.
10. Function is pure and stateless: no dictionary/irregular-word involvement. "Person"->"person", "Children"->"children", "Mice"->"mice", "Data"->"data", "Series"->"series", "Index"->"index", "Analyses"->"analyses" (all just a lowercased first letter). Passing the same word at argv[2] and argv[4] in one run gives identical output both times.
11. Driver line mapping CONFIRMED: underscore is emitted only for even argv indices (i%2==0), i.e. argv[2] and argv[4]. With 5 args the 15 output lines are: 1-2 = plural/camel(argv1), 3-5 = plural/camel/UNDERSCORE(argv2), 6-7 = plural/camel(argv3), 8-10 = plural/camel/UNDERSCORE(argv4), 11-12 = plural/camel(argv5), 13 = isPlural(argv1), 14 = isSingular(argv1), 15 = demodulize. With 4 args there are 13 lines (underscore at 5 and 10). A 6th argument is ignored entirely (i <= 5 cap). No args -> no output, exit 0.
12. Randomized differential test: 300 random strings over the alphabet [ABCXYZabcxyz0189_-.:/ ] (length 1-12) compared against the reference implementation above -> 0 mismatches.
13. All probes exited with status 0; nothing was ever written to stderr.

CLAIMED UNCERTAINTIES:
Byte 0x00 cannot be delivered through argv, so the behavior of a NUL byte inside the string is untestable via this driver. Given that all 255 other byte values follow the single rule and the function clearly iterates by size() rather than by C-string terminator (embedded 0x0A and 0xFF are handled fine), a 0x00 would almost certainly be copied verbatim as a non-uppercase byte — but this is inference, not measurement.
Whether the implementation literally calls std::toupper/std::tolower/std::isupper or does an inline range compare with +0x20 is indistinguishable black-box, because in the "C" locale they agree on every one of the 255 testable byte values. The observable mapping is exactly: 0x41-0x5A -> '_' + (byte+0x20) (with the leading '_' suppressed at index 0), everything else identity.
Whether the '_' + lowercase is produced by two separate appends or by appending a 2-char string is unobservable and irrelevant to the byte output.
Untested: inputs larger than ~6 KB were exercised (up to 5000 and 6000 bytes with correct lengths), but no multi-megabyte input was tried, so a pathological reallocation/limit above that size is not formally excluded (no evidence of any limit).

